<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gudea</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 2100 BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gudea was a ruler of the Sumerian city-state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagash">Lagash</a> around 2100 BCE, in the period between the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a> and the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Third Dynasty of Ur</a>. He is known for an unusual number of temple-building inscriptions and some two dozen diorite statues showing him in prayer, most of them now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudea-cylinders">Gudea Cylinders</a> describe the rebuilding of the Eninnu temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ningirsu">Ningirsu</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girsu">Girsu</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gudea.jpg" alt="Gudea" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jastrow · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gudea_of_Lagash_Girsu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
